###############################################################################
# The Custom-stage variant of stages 1 and 3 — the fallback, and the path that
# has actually been run.
#
# These two stages are a drop-in replacement for the IACM stages 1 and 3 in
# pipeline-ephemeral-db-ci.yaml. They run Terraform yourself, in a build pod in
# the target cluster, authenticating as that pod's ServiceAccount. Stage 2 is
# the same either way and is not reproduced here.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, stated plainly: this is the version proven end to end
# on a real Harness account against a real cluster — all three stages green,
# from an empty cluster to a destroyed namespace, on both migration tools
# (Flyway: provision 102s, destroy 81s; Liquibase: 94s and 52s — see
# ISSUES.md). The IACM stages now in the pipeline are schema-validated against
# Harness's published pipeline schema and reasoned from the docs, but they have
# never been executed on an account: no workspace was created and the Resources
# tab was never seen. If you need a green run today, start here.
#
# WHEN TO USE IT:
#   - Your account or your module licensing does not include IaCM.
#   - You want the path with a run history behind it rather than the one with a
#     schema check behind it.
#   - Your cluster cannot host Harness build pods at all, so you have to run on
#     Docker or Cloud runtime with env-var credentials. In that case see
#     terraform/bootstrap/provisioner-rbac.yaml, which carries an optional
#     named ServiceAccount and a long-lived token Secret for exactly that
#     case — the default harness-builds:default binding is an in-cluster POD
#     identity and gives you no token to paste anywhere.
#
# WHAT YOU GIVE UP by using it: the Resources tab, drift detection, cost
# estimation, the plan artifact you can gate with OPA or an approval, and
# platform-managed state. The last one is not a nicety — see the backend note
# below.
#
# THE TWO PATHS ARE MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE. These stages copy
# backend-kubernetes.tf.example into place, which puts a `backend "kubernetes"`
# block in terraform/. If an IaCM workspace ever points at a terraform/
# directory containing that block, Terraform honours the block and state goes
# to the Secret instead of to the platform — so IaCM's own state stays empty,
# its destroy plans against nothing, and you are back to the silent leak. Use
# one path or the other, not a mixture.
#
# ALSO REQUIRED IF YOU USE THESE STAGES
#
# 1. Re-add the CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64 pipeline variable. It was removed from the
#    main pipeline when stages 1 and 3 became IACM stages, because the two
#    Terraform Run steps below were its only consumers. The steps below still
#    reference <+pipeline.variables.CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64>, so without the variable
#    the pipeline will not save. Paste this back into pipeline.variables,
#    alongside MIGRATION_TOOL:
#
#     # Leave empty unless your egress goes through a TLS-inspecting proxy — the
#     # kind of corporate MITM appliance that presents its own certificate for
#     # every HTTPS host it re-signs. Your laptop and your delegate host
#     # trust the proxy's root CA because someone installed it there; a build
#     # container starts from the image's CA bundle and does not, so
#     # `terraform init` dies reaching registry.terraform.io with:
#     #
#     #   Failed to query available provider packages
#     #   ... tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: certificate signed by
#     #   unknown authority
#     #
#     # which reads like a Terraform or registry problem and is neither. Image
#     # pulls and git clone keep working, because the kubelet and the Harness
#     # steps use hosts and images that already trust the proxy — so only the
#     # Terraform steps fail, which points suspicion in the wrong direction.
#     #
#     # Base64, not raw PEM: a multi-line value would have to survive expression
#     # substitution into a shell script intact. Set it to a secret expression,
#     # e.g. <+secrets.getValue("custom_ca_pem_b64")>, holding the output of
#     #   base64 < your-root-ca.pem | tr -d '\n'
#     - name: CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64
#       type: String
#       description: "Optional. Base64 of a PEM CA bundle to trust, for TLS-inspecting proxies. Empty means no-op."
#       required: false
#       value: ""
#
#    The IaCM-native equivalent is the workspace environment variable
#    PLUGIN_CA_CERT_PATH, which is NOT a drop-in swap — it takes a filesystem
#    path to an already-mounted PEM bundle, not base64 of one. See ISSUES.md,
#    issue 14.
#
# 2. Keep terraform/backend-kubernetes.tf.example. It is what makes stage 3
#    honest: a destroy that cannot see stage 1's state plans nothing, prints
#    "No changes. No objects need to be destroyed.", exits 0, and leaves the
#    database running. That failure mode has been reproduced deliberately; it
#    is a green run that leaks.
#
#    Its secret_suffix is hardcoded to "ephemeral-db", so the state object is
#    always secret/tfstate-default-ephemeral-db in the build namespace. Every
#    consumer of this repo pointed at one cluster therefore shares ONE state
#    object. Two pipelines, two DB_NAMESPACE values, or two branches against
#    the same cluster will overwrite each other's state, and the loser's
#    destroy will plan against the winner's resources. The Lease only
#    serializes concurrent access; it does not separate unrelated states.
#    secret_suffix cannot be a variable inside a backend block, so the fix is
#    at init time:
#
#      terraform init -input=false \
#        -backend-config=secret_suffix=ephemeral-db-<something-unique>
#
#    Use the same value in both stages, or stage 3 destroys nothing. This whole
#    problem is specific to this file: on the IaCM path the platform owns state
#    per workspace and there is no shared Secret to collide over.
#
# 3. Everything the main pipeline already needs: the <<K8S_CONNECTOR>>,
#    <<GIT_CONNECTOR>> and <<DOCKER_CONNECTOR>> placeholders, and
#    terraform/bootstrap/provisioner-rbac.yaml applied once by a cluster admin.
#    These stages do NOT need <<IACM_WORKSPACE_ID>>; delete that variable from
#    the pipeline if neither remaining stage uses it.
###############################################################################

    ###########################################################################
    # STAGE 1 — Provision a database that did not exist 60 seconds ago.
    #
    # This runs Terraform in a build pod inside the target cluster, using the
    # pod's own ServiceAccount token — the same identity, in the same place, as
    # the IACM KubernetesDirect stage it replaces. The difference is who owns
    # the state and who renders the plan, not who authenticates.
    #
    # Note what is NOT the reason to be here. IaCM is not blocked on a private
    # cluster: Kubernetes build infrastructure is selected through
    # `infrastructure: KubernetesDirect`, not through `runtime`, whose enum is
    # the two-value [Docker, Cloud] that made this look impossible. See
    # ISSUES.md, issue 6, for the wrong turn and the correction.
    #
    # Prerequisite: terraform/bootstrap/provisioner-rbac.yaml, applied once by
    # a cluster admin. It grants this build pod's ServiceAccount the access
    # the Terraform needs. Without it the apply fails Forbidden on the first
    # namespace it tries to create.
    ###########################################################################
    - stage:
        name: Provision Ephemeral DB
        identifier: provision_ephemeral_db
        type: Custom
        spec:
          execution:
            steps:
              - stepGroup:
                  name: Provision
                  identifier: provision
                  stepGroupInfra:
                    type: KubernetesDirect
                    spec:
                      connectorRef: <<K8S_CONNECTOR>>
                      namespace: <<HARNESS_BUILD_NAMESPACE>>
                  steps:
                    - step:
                        type: GitClone
                        name: Clone Codebase
                        identifier: clone_codebase
                        spec:
                          connectorRef: <<GIT_CONNECTOR>>
                          repoName: <<REPO_NAME>>
                          build:
                            type: branch
                            spec:
                              branch: <+input>
                        timeout: 5m

                    - step:
                        type: Run
                        name: Terraform Apply
                        identifier: terraform_apply
                        spec:
                          connectorRef: <<DOCKER_CONNECTOR>>
                          # 1.5.7 is the last MPL-licensed release. 1.6.0+ is
                          # BSL and not supported here; OpenTofu works too.
                          image: hashicorp/terraform:1.5.7
                          shell: Sh
                          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
                          envVariables:
                            TF_IN_AUTOMATION: "true"
                            TF_VAR_db_password: <+secrets.getValue("ephemeral_db_password")>
                            TF_VAR_namespace: <<DB_NAMESPACE>>
                            TF_VAR_build_namespace: <<HARNESS_BUILD_NAMESPACE>>
                            CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64: <+pipeline.variables.CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64>
                          command: |
                            set -e

                            # No-op unless CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64 is set — see the
                            # pipeline variable for why this exists.
                            if [ -n "${CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64:-}" ]; then
                              echo "$CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64" | base64 -d \
                                >> /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
                              echo "Added a custom CA to the trust store."
                            fi

                            cd <<REPO_NAME>>/terraform

                            # Remote state in a Secret, so stage 3 can destroy
                            # what this stage created. Without it, destroy plans
                            # against empty state, reports no changes, exits
                            # green, and leaks the database.
                            cp backend-kubernetes.tf.example backend-kubernetes.tf

                            terraform init -input=false
                            terraform apply -input=false -auto-approve

                            echo "--- outputs ---"
                            terraform output
                        timeout: 15m
                        # Back-to-back runs race the previous run's teardown:
                        # deleting a namespace is asynchronous, so this apply
                        # can arrive while the old one is still Terminating and
                        # fail with "object is being deleted". Retrying is the
                        # whole fix — the namespace finishes terminating within
                        # seconds.
                        failureStrategies:
                          - onFailure:
                              errors:
                                - AllErrors
                              action:
                                type: Retry
                                spec:
                                  retryCount: 3
                                  retryIntervals:
                                    - 30s
                                    - 60s
                                    - 120s
                                  onRetryFailure:
                                    action:
                                      type: MarkAsFailure

    ###########################################################################
    # STAGE 3 — Destroy it. Unconditionally.
    #
    # `when.pipelineStatus: All` is doing real work here: without it, a failed
    # migration leaves the database running and you have reinvented the
    # shared staging database you were trying to escape.
    #
    # This stage is only honest if it shares state with stage 1. A destroy that
    # cannot see the previous apply's state plans nothing, prints "no changes",
    # and goes green while the database keeps running — a leak that looks like
    # a success. That is what backend-kubernetes.tf.example is for, and why
    # both stages copy it into place before init.
    ###########################################################################
    - stage:
        name: Destroy Ephemeral DB
        identifier: destroy_ephemeral_db
        type: Custom
        when:
          pipelineStatus: All
        spec:
          execution:
            steps:
              - stepGroup:
                  name: Destroy
                  identifier: destroy
                  stepGroupInfra:
                    type: KubernetesDirect
                    spec:
                      connectorRef: <<K8S_CONNECTOR>>
                      namespace: <<HARNESS_BUILD_NAMESPACE>>
                  steps:
                    - step:
                        type: GitClone
                        name: Clone Codebase
                        identifier: clone_codebase_destroy
                        spec:
                          connectorRef: <<GIT_CONNECTOR>>
                          repoName: <<REPO_NAME>>
                          build:
                            type: branch
                            spec:
                              branch: <+input>
                        timeout: 5m

                    - step:
                        type: Run
                        name: Terraform Destroy
                        identifier: terraform_destroy
                        spec:
                          connectorRef: <<DOCKER_CONNECTOR>>
                          image: hashicorp/terraform:1.5.7
                          shell: Sh
                          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
                          envVariables:
                            TF_IN_AUTOMATION: "true"
                            # Required even to destroy: the variable has no
                            # default, and Terraform validates variables before
                            # it will plan a destroy.
                            TF_VAR_db_password: <+secrets.getValue("ephemeral_db_password")>
                            TF_VAR_namespace: <<DB_NAMESPACE>>
                            TF_VAR_build_namespace: <<HARNESS_BUILD_NAMESPACE>>
                            CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64: <+pipeline.variables.CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64>
                          command: |
                            set -e
                            if [ -n "${CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64:-}" ]; then
                              echo "$CUSTOM_CA_PEM_B64" | base64 -d \
                                >> /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
                            fi
                            cd <<REPO_NAME>>/terraform
                            cp backend-kubernetes.tf.example backend-kubernetes.tf
                            terraform init -input=false
                            terraform destroy -input=false -auto-approve
                            echo "Ephemeral database destroyed."
                        timeout: 15m
